            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Noodles gone mushy",
              "cause": "Cooked too long",
              "fix": "Add noodles in the last 8 minutes only."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Pistou tastes bitter",
              "cause": "Basil was pounded when the mortar was too warm",
              "fix": "Let the mortar cool before pounding."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Soupe au pistou is Provence's answer to minestrone. The name pistou comes from the Provencal word for pounding. Families make this soup at the height of summer using whatever vegetables are in the garden. It is served at room temperature or slightly warm, never piping hot. The pistou is always added at the table, not during cooking, to keep its fresh flavour."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
